{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For fluid pressure: identify depth (m), fluid density (kg/m³), and g, then calculate p = hρg",
   "For total pressure: add atmospheric pressure (≈ 101,000 Pa) to the fluid pressure",
   "For upthrust: upthrust = ρ_fluid × V_submerged × g",
   "Compare upthrust with weight to decide: float (upthrust ≥ weight) or sink (upthrust < weight)"
  ],
  "title": "Fluid Pressure and Upthrust",
  "content": "<p>Two calculation types for this lesson.</p><p><strong>Pressure due to a fluid column:</strong> \\(p = h\\rho g\\)</p><p>Where h = depth (m), ρ = fluid density (kg/m³), g = gravitational field strength (N/kg). This gives the pressure from the fluid only. To find total pressure at depth, add atmospheric pressure: p_total = p_atm + hρg.</p><p><strong>Upthrust:</strong> Upthrust = ρ_fluid × V_displaced × g</p><p>Where V_displaced = the volume of the submerged object (or the submerged portion if partly above the surface). Compare upthrust with the object's weight to determine floating or sinking.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>p = hρg gives fluid pressure only. Add atmospheric pressure when the question asks for total pressure</li><li>Upthrust uses the density of the fluid, not the object</li><li>If upthrust &gt; weight, the object rises (floats); if upthrust &lt; weight, it sinks; if equal, it is in equilibrium</li></ul>"
 },
 "topic_links": {
  "prerequisites": []
 },
 "exam_context": {
  "marks": "2-4 marks per calculation",
  "paper": "Physics (Higher Tier)",
  "frequency": "Moderate — fluid pressure and upthrust appear regularly in Separate Physics"
 },
